                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238492839",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198717011612",
                            "num_reviews": 2,
                            "playtime_forever": 201528,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 11,
                            "playtime_at_review": 201516,
                            "last_played": 1793235287
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The developers once banned a ton of people because they supposedly had duplicated the strongest armor in the game. You can obtain it during an event, but the drop rate is very low. Otherwise, you have to slowly gather the materials to craft it, and crafting or buying it costs a huge amount of in-game currency.\n\nThe thing is, the game has something called an Auction House. Players can anonymously list their items either for time-limited auctions or with a fixed buyout price. The duplicated armor was eventually tracked down, and the accounts that had it were banned.\n\nIt didn't matter if you knew where the armor came from. It didn't matter if you had bought it through the Auction House, a place moderated by the developers themselves. You could still get banned just for owning it. In the best-case scenario, they would take the armor away from you, but they wouldn't refund the money you had spent on it.\n\nAnyway, I spent a whole year farming to build up a large amount of energy in the game (a currency that you can buy with real money but can also exchange for regular in-game currency). Back when 100 energy cost around 8,000 crowns (crowns being the standard in-game currency), I loaned a friend around 42,000 energy. It took me two years and a huge amount of my free time to accumulate all of that.\n\nMy friend got banned.\n\nI did everything I possibly could to contact the developers, providing all the evidence I had, but they told me it was my problem and that I shouldn't have lent him the money in the first place.\n\nI still play the game to this day, but keep in mind that if you try to file a complaint through Steam Support, they'll redirect you straight to the developers without acting as an intermediary.\n\nIf anyone from Steam happens to read this... well, I don't even know what to say.",
